<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shang Yang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 390–<a href="/years/338-bce">338 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Lord Shang, Wei Yang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shang Yang was a Chinese statesman and Legalist thinker, a nobleman of the State of Wey, who served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiao-of-qin">Duke Xiao of Qin</a> from 361 BCE. His reforms abolished hereditary privilege, rewarded farming and military merit, imposed harsh laws with collective responsibility and organised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> into counties. They turned Qin into a centralised war state that later unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. After Duke Xiao's death he was executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shang-yang.jpg" alt="Shang Yang" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taken by Fanghong · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Shang_Yang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shang Yang, also called Wei Yang, was originally surnamed Gongsun. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> he was the son of a concubine of the ruling ducal family of the minor state of Wey. As a young man he studied law and obtained a position under Prime Minister Shuzuo of the state of Wei, then left that lowly post, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duke-xiao-of-qin">Duke Xiao of Qin</a>, to become chief adviser in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. He served as Qin's chief minister for twenty years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He launched two series of reforms. The first, in 356 BCE, put into force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kui">Li Kui</a>'s Book of Law, with the addition of a rule punishing those who knew of a crime and failed to inform the government. Land was assigned to soldiers according to their military success, nobles unwilling to fight lost their land rights, and the army was divided into twenty grades according to battlefield achievement. The cultivation of wasteland and immigration were encouraged, and agriculture was favoured over luxury commerce. The second set, in 350 BCE, brought a standardised system of land allocation and tax reforms.</p><p>Most of the measures were taken from policies tried elsewhere, such as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-qi">Wu Qi</a> of Chu, but his were more thorough and extreme and placed policy in the ruler's hands. He partly abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primogeniture">primogeniture</a> and doubled the tax on households with more than one adult son, to break large clans into nuclear families. He also moved the capital from Yueyang to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a> to reduce the influence of nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> nobility despised Shang Yang, and he was vulnerable after the death of Duke Xiao. The next ruler, King Huiwen, ordered the nine familial exterminations against him on grounds of fomenting rebellion. He was executed by being torn apart by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a>, and his family was killed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> tells it differently: he fled to Wei, was expelled, raised a rebel army in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> and died in battle. King Huiwen kept his reforms.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a> credits him with the theories of fixing the standards and equality before the law, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-lord-shang">Book of Lord Shang</a> is likely the work of both him and his followers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a> reports that after ten years of the ordinances the commoners of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> were delighted, and that no one picked up lost articles on the road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shang Yang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 359 BCE and his execution in 338 BCE the statesman <b>Shang Yang</b> introduced the reforms that made Qin a centralised state with a large army, and helped build the capital at Xianyang on the Wei River, in the Guanzhong heartland of fertile farmland protected by mountains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Shang Yang</b>'s reforms from 356 BCE transformed society.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hanshu took Li Kui as the first author of works on regulations and an influence on <b>Shang Yang</b>, under whose reforms in the 4th century BCE Legalist ideas were first adopted by a state, Qin.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Confucius spent years there, and the reformer <b>Shang Yang</b> was a Wey nobleman.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-wey">State of Wey</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puyang">Puyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in Xianyang, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.76,115.03&amp;year=-357" aria-label="Puyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Shang Yang"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4656 4500h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4656 4500h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.76,115.03&amp;year=-357">What was here in 357 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
